{% extends "admin/layout.twig" %}
{% block topbar_eyebrow %}<small>Judging</small>{% endblock %}
{% block topbar_title %}Judging rubric{% endblock %}

{#
  ── WHAT THIS SCREEN IS FOR ──────────────────────────────────────────────────

  `gates_judge_criteria` is the table the whole scoring system runs on — every
  ballot, every weighted average, every bias check, every published result — and
  until this screen existed there was NO WAY TO EDIT IT from anywhere. It was
  written by the installer and by the sandbox seeder and read everywhere else.

  On a platform whose integrity argument is "the criteria are fixed and published
  before judging starts", that is not a missing convenience.
#}

{% set scope_label = scope ? (programmes|filter(p => p.id == scope)|first).title|default('this programme') : 'Every programme' %}

{% block content %}

<p style="margin:0 0 14px;font-size:13.5px;line-height:1.7;color:var(--ad-text-mute,#5a6d6f)">
  What a judge is asked about each nominee, and what each answer is worth. This is the rubric
  the panel scores against and the one the published result is computed from.
</p>

{# ── THE QUESTION THE FOUR CRITERIA ANSWER ────────────────────────────────────
   Four criteria listed on their own read as a scoring form. With the question above them
   they read as a standard, which is what they are — and an operator about to reweight one
   should have the reason for the weights in front of them while they do it. #}
<div class="ad-card">
  <div class="ad-card__body" style="padding-top:18px;padding-bottom:18px">
    <p style="margin:0;font-family:var(--ag-font-display,Georgia,serif);font-size:16.5px;line-height:1.6;color:var(--ad-text,#10292c)">
      &ldquo;{{ purpose }}&rdquo;
    </p>
    <p style="margin:12px 0 0;font-size:12.5px;line-height:1.7;color:var(--ad-text-mute,#5a6d6f)">
      Four criteria, <strong>equally weighted</strong> — so no single dimension can carry a
      result on its own: not wealth, not a following, not the public tally, not a reputation
      that arrived before the evidence did. A rubric weighted 40/25/20/15 lets its heaviest
      criterion decide, and the heaviest criterion is always the one easiest to perform.
    </p>
    <p style="margin:10px 0 0;font-size:12.5px;line-height:1.7;color:var(--ad-text-mute,#5a6d6f)">
      Weights here are <strong>relative</strong>, so four 25s and four 1s score identically.
      25 is used because the rubric is published as percentages and the number on this screen
      should be the number that was published.
    </p>
  </div>
</div>

{# ── NO RUBRIC AT ALL ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   JudgeService LOCKS scoring for a programme with no criteria, so an empty global rubric is
   not a blank slate — it is a panel that cannot score anybody. Offered only when the global
   rubric is genuinely empty; the seeder refuses over existing rows regardless, because those
   rows may already have ballots pointing at them. #}
{% if can_install|default(false) and may_edit %}
<div class="ad-card" style="border-color:rgba(163,43,28,.3)">
  <div class="ad-card__head"><div class="ad-card__title" style="color:#a32b1c">There is no rubric
    <small>Judges cannot score anybody until there is one — the panel screen locks without criteria.</small>
  </div></div>
  <div class="ad-card__body">
    <p style="margin:0 0 12px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.7">
      Install the four published criteria — <strong>Impact</strong>, <strong>Originality</strong>,
      <strong>Reach</strong> and <strong>Integrity</strong>, 25 each. You can edit or reweight
      any of them afterwards; nothing here is final until judging starts.
    </p>
    <form method="post" action="/admin/rubric/install">
      <input type="hidden" name="_token" value="{{ csrf_token }}">
      <button class="ad-btn ad-btn--primary" type="submit"
        data-confirm="Install the four published criteria at 25 each? They become the default rubric for every programme, and you can edit, reweight or retire any of them afterwards.">Install the published rubric</button>
    </form>
  </div>
</div>
{% endif %}

{# ── SCOPE ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     Global rows apply to every programme; a programme's own row with the same
     reference REPLACES the global one. That is what the scorer already does — this
     screen shows the resolution rather than the raw rows, because an invisible
     override is how a programme ends up judged on criteria nobody meant to give it. #}
<div class="ad-card">
  <div class="ad-card__head"><div class="ad-card__title">Which rubric</div></div>
  <div class="ad-card__body">
    <form method="get" action="/admin/rubric" class="ad-form">
      <div class="row">
        <div>
          <label for="rbScope">Rubric for</label>
          <select id="rbScope" name="programme" data-ag-do="submit-form">
            <option value="global" {{ scope is null ? 'selected' : '' }}>Every programme (the default rubric)</option>
            {% for p in programmes %}
              <option value="{{ p.id }}" {{ scope == p.id ? 'selected' : '' }}>{{ p.title }}</option>
            {% endfor %}
          </select>
        </div>
        <div style="align-self:end">
          <noscript><button class="ad-btn ad-btn--sm" type="submit">Show</button></noscript>
        </div>
      </div>
    </form>
    {% if scope %}
      <p style="margin:10px 0 0;font-size:12.5px;line-height:1.7;color:var(--ad-text-soft)">
        A criterion here <strong>replaces</strong> the default one with the same reference for
        {{ scope_label }}. Anything the default has and this does not is still asked.</p>
    {% endif %}
  </div>
</div>

{# ── WHAT CHANGING IT COSTS ────────────────────────────────────────────────
     Not a lock. An operator may have a real reason to change a rubric mid-cycle, and a
     platform that refuses is one they work around in the database. But scores already
     recorded were given against the OLD weights, and the published result moves under
     them — so they are told, with the number. #}
{% if exposure.scored %}
<div class="ad-card" style="border-color:#ecd9a0;background:#fffdf5">
  <div class="ad-card__body" style="font-size:13.5px;line-height:1.7;color:#6b5312">
    <p style="margin:0">{{ exposure.note }}</p>

    {# ── AND ADDING ONE IS THE DANGEROUS EDIT ────────────────────────────────
       A scorecard counts toward quorum only when it covers every ACTIVE criterion, which
       is the right rule and has a sharp edge: completeness is measured against the rubric
       as it stands NOW. Adding a fifth criterion to a rubric of four instantly makes every
       existing scorecard incomplete — every judge is missing the new one — so they stop
       counting toward quorum, nominees drop below it, and the judge component leaves the
       CPI entirely.

       Measured: two complete scorecards, quorum met, nominee eligible. Add one criterion
       and it is judges 0, eligible NO, CPI back to the community-only figure. Nothing
       errors and nothing is logged; the operator's next sight of it is a category that
       suddenly cannot crown anybody.

       Retiring is the safe direction — the required set shrinks, and a scorecard that
       covered five still covers the remaining four. #}
    {% if exposure.scorecards|default(0) > 0 %}
      <p style="margin:12px 0 0;padding-top:12px;border-top:1px solid #ecd9a0">
        <strong>Adding a criterion would void
        {{ exposure.scorecards }} completed scorecard{{ exposure.scorecards == 1 ? '' : 's' }}.</strong>
        A scorecard counts only when it covers every criterion, so a new one leaves every
        judge one short — they stop counting toward quorum, and any nominee who falls
        below it loses the judge half of their score until the panel scores the new
        criterion for everybody.
        <span style="display:block;margin-top:6px">Retiring a criterion is safe: the
          remaining marks still cover what is left.</span>
      </p>
    {% endif %}
  </div>
</div>
{% endif %}

{# ══ WHAT A PANEL IS ACTUALLY ASKED ═══════════════════════════════════════ #}
<div class="ad-card">
  <div class="ad-card__head"><div class="ad-card__title">
    What a judge sees<small>After the default and this programme are resolved against each other</small>
  </div></div>

  {% if effective %}
  <div class="ad-table-wrap">
    <table class="ad-table">
      <caption class="sr-only">The rubric a panel is scored against</caption>
      <thead><tr>
        <th scope="col">Criterion</th><th scope="col">Reference</th>
        <th scope="col">Weight</th><th scope="col">Share of the score</th><th scope="col">From</th>
      </tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {% for c in effective %}
        <tr>
          <td><strong>{{ c.label }}</strong>
            {% if c.description %}<div style="font-size:12.5px;color:var(--ad-text-soft);margin-top:3px">{{ c.description }}</div>{% endif %}</td>
          <td><code>{{ c.slug }}</code></td>
          <td>{{ c.weight }}</td>
          {# The number an operator actually wants, and the one they would otherwise work
             out wrongly in their head. The stored weight is RELATIVE. #}
          <td><strong>{{ shares[c.id]|default(0) }}%</strong></td>
          <td>{% if c.programme_id %}<span class="ad-chip ad-chip--green">This programme</span>
              {% else %}<span class="ad-chip ad-chip--grey">Default</span>{% endif %}</td>
        </tr>
        {% endfor %}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="ad-card__body" style="border-top:1px solid var(--ad-line);font-size:13px;line-height:1.7;color:var(--ad-text-soft)">
    <strong>Weights are relative, not percentages.</strong> The score is divided by the total
    of whatever is active, so four criteria at 25 and four at 1 give exactly the same result.
    They do not need to add up to anything — the share column above is what each one is
    actually worth.
  </div>
  {% else %}
  <div class="ad-card__body" style="font-size:13.5px;line-height:1.7;color:var(--ad-text-soft)">
    <strong>There is no rubric yet, so scoring is locked.</strong> A judge cannot open a
    ballot with nothing to score against. Add at least one criterion below.
  </div>
  {% endif %}
</div>

{# ══ THE ROWS IN THIS SCOPE ═══════════════════════════════════════════════ #}
<div class="ad-card">
  <div class="ad-card__head"><div class="ad-card__title">
    {{ scope ? scope_label ~ ' — its own criteria' : 'The default criteria' }}
    <small>{{ rows|length }} of {{ max_per_scope }}</small>
  </div></div>

  {% if rows %}
  <div class="ad-table-wrap">
    <table class="ad-table">
      <caption class="sr-only">Criteria in this rubric</caption>
      <thead><tr>
        <th scope="col">Criterion</th><th scope="col">Weight</th>
        <th scope="col">Order</th><th scope="col">Scored</th><th scope="col">Change it</th>
      </tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {% for r in rows %}
        <tr>
          <td>
            <strong>{{ r.row.label }}</strong>
            {% if not r.row.is_active %}<span class="ad-chip ad-chip--grey">Retired</span>{% endif %}
            <div style="font-size:12.5px;color:var(--ad-text-soft);margin-top:3px">
              <code>{{ r.row.slug }}</code>{% if r.row.description %} · {{ r.row.description }}{% endif %}</div>
          </td>
          <td>{{ r.row.weight }}</td>
          <td>{{ r.row.sort_order }}</td>
          {# Shown BEFORE anything is pressed. It is what decides whether this can be
             deleted or only retired, and the operator cannot run that query themselves. #}
          <td>{{ r.scores > 0 ? r.scores|number_format : '—' }}</td>
          <td>
            {% if may_edit %}
            <details>
              <summary class="ad-btn ad-btn--sm" style="cursor:pointer">Edit</summary>
              <form class="ad-form" method="post"
                    action="/admin/rubric/{{ r.row.id }}{{ scope ? '?programme=' ~ scope : '' }}"
                    style="margin-top:10px">
                <input type="hidden" name="_token" value="{{ csrf_token }}">
                <div class="row">
                  <div><label for="l{{ r.row.id }}">Name</label>
                    <input id="l{{ r.row.id }}" name="label" type="text" required maxlength="160"
                           value="{{ r.row.label }}"></div>
                  <div><label for="w{{ r.row.id }}">Weight</label>
                    <input id="w{{ r.row.id }}" name="weight" type="number" min="1"
                           max="{{ max_weight }}" required value="{{ r.row.weight }}"></div>
                </div>
                <div class="row">
                  <div><label for="s{{ r.row.id }}">Order</label>
                    <input id="s{{ r.row.id }}" name="sort_order" type="number" min="0" max="999"
                           value="{{ r.row.sort_order }}"></div>
                  <div><label for="g{{ r.row.id }}">Reference</label>
                    {# Locked once scored: the scorer resolves the per-programme override BY
                       SLUG, so a rename silently re-points it at nothing. #}
                    <input id="g{{ r.row.id }}" name="slug" type="text" maxlength="40"
                           value="{{ r.row.slug }}" {{ r.scores > 0 ? 'readonly' : '' }}>
                    {% if r.scores > 0 %}<small style="color:var(--ad-text-soft)">Locked — ballots point at it.</small>{% endif %}
                  </div>
                </div>
                <div>
                  <label for="d{{ r.row.id }}">What a judge should look for</label>
                  <textarea id="d{{ r.row.id }}" name="description" rows="2"
                            maxlength="600">{{ r.row.description }}</textarea>
                </div>
                <div style="margin-top:10px;display:flex;gap:9px;flex-wrap:wrap">
                  <button class="ad-btn ad-btn--sm ad-btn--primary" type="submit">Save</button>
                </div>
              </form>

              <div style="margin-top:10px;display:flex;gap:9px;flex-wrap:wrap">
                {% if r.row.is_active %}
                  <form method="post"
                        action="/admin/rubric/{{ r.row.id }}/retire{{ scope ? '?programme=' ~ scope : '' }}"
                        data-confirm="{% if r.scores > 0 %}Retire “{{ r.row.label }}”? It stops being asked. The {{ r.scores|number_format }} score{{ r.scores == 1 ? '' : 's' }} already recorded against it are kept — deleting them would change results that have already been published.{% else %}Remove “{{ r.row.label }}”? Nothing has been scored against it, so it will be deleted.{% endif %}">
                    <input type="hidden" name="_token" value="{{ csrf_token }}">
                    <button class="ad-btn ad-btn--sm" type="submit">
                      {{ r.scores > 0 ? 'Retire it' : 'Remove it' }}</button>
                  </form>
                {% else %}
                  <form method="post"
                        action="/admin/rubric/{{ r.row.id }}/restore{{ scope ? '?programme=' ~ scope : '' }}">
                    <input type="hidden" name="_token" value="{{ csrf_token }}">
                    <button class="ad-btn ad-btn--sm" type="submit">Put it back</button>
                  </form>
                {% endif %}
              </div>
            </details>
            {% else %}
              <span style="color:var(--ad-text-soft)">Read only</span>
            {% endif %}
          </td>
        </tr>
        {% endfor %}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  {% else %}
  <div class="ad-card__body" style="font-size:13.5px;line-height:1.7;color:var(--ad-text-soft)">
    {% if scope %}
      Nothing specific to {{ scope_label }}. Its panel is asked the default criteria above,
      which is usually right — add one here only when this programme needs to be judged
      differently.
    {% else %}
      No criteria yet. Scoring is locked until there is at least one.
    {% endif %}
  </div>
  {% endif %}
</div>

{# ══ ADD ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ #}
{% if may_edit and rows|length < max_per_scope %}
<div class="ad-card">
  <div class="ad-card__head"><div class="ad-card__title">Add a criterion
    <small>{{ scope ? 'For ' ~ scope_label ~ ' only' : 'For every programme' }}</small>
  </div></div>
  <div class="ad-card__body">
    <form class="ad-form" method="post" action="/admin/rubric{{ scope ? '?programme=' ~ scope : '' }}">
      <input type="hidden" name="_token" value="{{ csrf_token }}">
      <div class="row">
        <div><label for="nLabel">Name <span aria-hidden="true">*</span></label>
          <input id="nLabel" name="label" type="text" required maxlength="160"
                 placeholder="Documented impact"></div>
        <div><label for="nWeight">Weight <span aria-hidden="true">*</span></label>
          <input id="nWeight" name="weight" type="number" min="1" max="{{ max_weight }}"
                 required value="25">
          <small style="color:var(--ad-text-soft)">Relative to the others, not a percentage.</small></div>
      </div>
      <div>
        <label for="nDesc">What a judge should look for</label>
        <textarea id="nDesc" name="description" rows="2" maxlength="600"
                  placeholder="Numbers and names. What changed, over what period, and who else can confirm it."></textarea>
      </div>
      <div style="margin-top:10px">
        <button class="ad-btn ad-btn--primary" type="submit">Add it to the rubric</button>
      </div>
    </form>
  </div>
</div>
{% endif %}

{# ── THE INDEPENDENCE PRINCIPLE ───────────────────────────────────────────────
   Printed here because it is a description of the CODE, not an aspiration — every clause
   below is enforced at a named place, and a principle that is only printed is one that
   quietly stops being true. See JudgeRubricSeeder::doctrine() for where each is enforced.

   It belongs beside the criteria rather than in a document elsewhere: an operator
   reweighting a criterion is deciding what the panel is asked, and the safeguards on HOW
   they are asked it are part of the same decision. #}
<div class="ad-card">
  <div class="ad-card__head"><div class="ad-card__title">The independence principle
    <small>Not policy — this is what the judging screen actually does.</small>
  </div></div>
  <div class="ad-card__body">
    {% for d in doctrine %}
      <div style="padding:12px 0{{ loop.first ? ';padding-top:0' : '' }}{{ loop.last ? '' : ';border-bottom:1px solid var(--ad-line)' }}">
        <p style="margin:0 0 4px;font-weight:600;font-size:13.5px;color:var(--ad-text,#10292c)">{{ d.title }}</p>
        <p style="margin:0;font-size:12.5px;line-height:1.7;color:var(--ad-text-mute,#5a6d6f)">{{ d.body }}</p>
      </div>
    {% endfor %}
    <p style="margin:16px 0 0;padding-top:14px;border-top:1px solid var(--ad-line);
              font-family:var(--ag-font-display,Georgia,serif);font-size:15px;line-height:1.6;color:var(--ad-text,#10292c)">
      &ldquo;{{ standard }}&rdquo;
    </p>
    <p style="margin:8px 0 0;font-size:12.5px;line-height:1.7;color:var(--ad-text-mute,#5a6d6f)">
      A nominee does not win because they are the most popular. They win because, across all
      four, their contribution withstands scrutiny.
    </p>
  </div>
</div>

{% endblock %}
